{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R66-pending-c — keeps serving. The half a naive implementation fails, measured in the one",
    "way §4.10(a) leaves available.",
    "",
    "THIRD OBLIGATION UNDER §9.1's LAST ROW. See ECP-R66-pending-b's header for why the id is arch's."
  ],
  "id_status": "pending-split",
  "under": "ECP-R66",
  "split_index": "c",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F3",
  "title": "After refusing over-limit input, the peer continues to serve",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §4.10 (\"Rejection is clean, not collapse\")",
  "spec_also": [
    "§4.9 (the resilience floor this composes with)",
    "§4.10(a)",
    "§4.10(b)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "reading": [
    "§4.10: \"hitting a bound returns the coded error (or refuses the connection) and the peer keeps",
    "serving -- it MUST NOT crash or degrade service to in-flight requests (composes with §4.9).\"",
    "",
    "⭐ THE WHOLE DIFFICULTY IS WHAT \"KEEPS SERVING\" RANGES OVER, and getting it wrong produces a check",
    "that fails conformant peers. Two readings:",
    "",
    "  (i)  the CONNECTION survives the refusal and remains usable;",
    "  (ii) the PEER remains available and continues to answer.",
    "",
    "(i) IS EXCLUDED BY §4.10(a) ITSELF. Its emission shape permits the peer to \"close the connection",
    "after a best-effort coded frame\" when the over-size condition is detected before `request_id` is",
    "parsed. A requirement demanding same-connection survival would therefore score a peer non-conformant",
    "for taking a path the same clause explicitly allows -- and §4.10's own parenthetical says the same",
    "thing in three words: \"(or refuses the connection)\".",
    "",
    "So this requirement is (ii), and it is measured ON A FRESH CONNECTION. That is not a weakening: (ii)",
    "is the property §4.9 cares about and the one an operator cares about. A peer that dies, wedges, or",
    "stops accepting connections after an over-limit frame fails this, and no reading of §4.10 saves it.",
    "",
    "WHERE THE SAME CONNECTION *IS* MEASURABLE: when the peer answered with a correlated 413 and did NOT",
    "close, the connection is live by construction, and a suite MAY additionally assert continued service",
    "on it. That is an observation, not the requirement -- a peer is not more conformant for keeping the",
    "connection.",
    "",
    "\"MUST NOT ... degrade service to in-flight requests\" is the second half and it needs a concurrent",
    "in-flight request to observe. It is a REAL obligation and this file does not measure it; it is",
    "carried as a declared exclusion below rather than dropped, because §4.9's `concurrency` surface is",
    "where it belongs and authoring it here would produce a load test wearing a conformance check's",
    "clothes."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "declared_limits": [
      "max_payload"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "declared_limits"
    ]
  },
  "declared_exclusions": {
    "in_flight_degradation": "§4.10's 'MUST NOT degrade service to in-flight requests' is not measured here — it needs a concurrent in-flight request and belongs with the §4.9 / concurrency surface. Declared rather than dropped: an unstated omission is indistinguishable from nobody having looked."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "still-serving",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "an over-limit refusal has just occurred (by either permitted emission shape)",
      "why": "§4.10's MUST. The fresh connection is what makes this measurable against BOTH conformant shapes.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "over",
          "size": "max_payload + 1",
          "note": "the refusal itself is ECP-R66's subject, not this one's. Any of its conformant outcomes may occur here."
        },
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "as": "fresh",
          "note": "A NEW CONNECTION, deliberately. §4.10(a) permits the peer to have closed the first one, so reusing it would make this requirement unpassable for a conformant peer."
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "after",
          "connection": "fresh",
          "note": "the smallest well-formed request the posture can make succeed — its subject is that the peer ANSWERS, not what it answers."
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "answered",
          "capture": "after",
          "why": "'keeps serving' is answered-at-all. Asserting 200 here would silently require a grant covering the follow-up request, making this requirement fail on posture rather than on resilience — which is exactly the class of defect this repo was founded on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same fresh-connection request issued with NO preceding over-limit frame",
      "why": "MANDATORY, and here it is load-bearing rather than ceremonial: it establishes that the follow-up request is answerable AT ALL under this posture. Without it, a peer that never answers this request — for grant, routing, or handler reasons having nothing to do with resource bounds — is indistinguishable from one the over-limit frame killed.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "baseline"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "answered",
          "capture": "baseline",
          "why": "if this fails, the run reports SKIP for the whole requirement — the instrument could not establish its own antecedent (GUIDE-CONFORMANCE §2.4b)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that exits, wedges, or stops accepting connections on an over-limit frame",
      "expect": "the fresh connection is refused or the request goes unanswered, while the control arm succeeded",
      "why": "the self-DoS §4.10 exists to prevent: a bound that turns an oversize frame into a kill switch has moved the vulnerability rather than closed it."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "resource_bounds/r1_payload_over_limit",
    "oracle_note": "the reference oracle appears to fold keeps-serving INTO r1 rather than scoring it separately; provenance only, and see ECP-R66's predicted disagreement."
  }
}
